---
name: Dataverse metadata API — gotchas from first-hand use
description: Lessons learned from creating tables/columns/relationships via the Web API metadata endpoints on 2026-04-11. Use these to avoid failed calls next time. Metadata cache propagation, schema name casing, throttling on rapid customizations.
type: feedback
---

Lessons from building `dcfg_customer_vendor` + `dcfg_property_vendor` via the Dataverse Web API on 2026-04-11. These are reusable notes for any future metadata work through `api/data/v9.2/EntityDefinitions` etc.

## 1. SchemaName casing determines LogicalName — use underscores, not CamelCase

**Why:** Dataverse computes `LogicalName` by lowercasing `SchemaName`. It does NOT insert word boundaries. `dcfg_CustomerVendor` becomes `dcfg_customervendor` (no underscore between customer and vendor), not `dcfg_customer_vendor`.

**How to apply:** Use SchemaNames with underscores already in them where you want underscores in the LogicalName.
- WANT `dcfg_customer_vendor` → use SchemaName `dcfg_Customer_Vendor`
- WANT `dcfg_active_flag` → use SchemaName `dcfg_Active_Flag`
- WANT `dcfg_assigned_date` → use SchemaName `dcfg_Assigned_Date`

The DCFG codebase convention is underscores between words (e.g., `dcfg_active_flag`, `dcfg_signer_name`, `dcfg_insurance_expiration_date`). Stay consistent.

## 2. Metadata cache propagation — poll before the next write

**Why:** After creating an entity via `POST /EntityDefinitions`, the API returns `204 NoContent` immediately, but the metadata cache (`LazyDynamicMetadataCache`) may not yet contain the new entity. Subsequent writes that reference the new entity (e.g., creating a relationship on it) fail with `0x80041102 "Could not find an entity with specified entity logicalname"` or `0x80041102 "was not found in the MetadataCache"`.

**How to apply:** After any `POST /EntityDefinitions` call, poll `GET /EntityDefinitions(LogicalName='...')` until it returns 200. Don't hardcode a sleep — it can take anywhere from 0-30s. Poll with 2-second intervals, give up after 60s and throw.

```powershell
function WaitForEntity([string]$logicalName) {
  $url = $OrgUri + "api/data/v9.2/EntityDefinitions(LogicalName='$logicalName')?`$select=LogicalName"
  for ($i = 0; $i -lt 30; $i++) {
    try {
      $r = Invoke-RestMethod -Uri $url -Headers $readH -Method Get
      if ($r.LogicalName -eq $logicalName) { return }
    } catch { }
    Start-Sleep -Seconds 2
  }
  throw "Entity $logicalName never became visible"
}
```

## 3. Entity customization throttling — 429 on rapid consecutive metadata writes

**Why:** Dataverse throttles concurrent `EntityCustomization` operations. Error: `0x80071151 "Cannot start another [EntityCustomization] because there is a previous [EntityCustomization] running at this moment"`. Triggered by rapid successive attribute creates/deletes against the same entity.

**How to apply:** Build a retry-with-backoff around every metadata POST/DELETE call. Wait 15 seconds on 429, retry up to 5 times. Throttling cleared in 1-2 attempts during the 2026-04-11 work.

```powershell
for ($i = 0; $i -lt 5; $i++) {
  try {
    $resp = Invoke-WebRequest ...
    break
  } catch {
    if ($i -eq 4) { throw }
    if ($_.Exception.Response.StatusCode -eq 429) { Start-Sleep -Seconds 15 }
    else { throw }
  }
}
```

## 4. Solution-scoped writes — always set MSCRM.SolutionUniqueName header

**Why:** Without it, new metadata is created outside any solution and can't be exported to other environments.

**How to apply:** Every metadata POST should carry `MSCRM.SolutionUniqueName: DCFGSystemTest` (or whichever solution). This is a request header, not a body field. The solution must exist first (query `/solutions?$filter=uniquename eq 'DCFGSystemTest'` to verify).

## 5. Delete-and-recreate is cheap for empty tables

**Why:** Dataverse doesn't support renaming tables or attributes after creation. If you get the name wrong on a fresh, empty entity, it's faster to `DELETE /EntityDefinitions(LogicalName='xxx')` and recreate than to leave inconsistent naming.

**How to apply:** Only use this while the table has zero data. `DELETE /EntityDefinitions(LogicalName='...')` removes the entity entirely. Recreate with correct SchemaName. Individual attributes can also be deleted via `DELETE /EntityDefinitions(LogicalName='x')/Attributes(LogicalName='y')`.

## 6. Labels are verbose objects, not strings

**Why:** Dataverse metadata API requires localized label objects, not bare strings.

**How to apply:** Every `DisplayName`, `DisplayCollectionName`, `Description`, `Label`, and `OptionSet.Options[].Label` field needs this shape:

```powershell
@{
  '@odata.type' = 'Microsoft.Dynamics.CRM.Label'
  LocalizedLabels = @(
    @{ '@odata.type' = 'Microsoft.Dynamics.CRM.LocalizedLabel'; Label = 'Display Name Here'; LanguageCode = 1033 }
  )
}
```

Wrap it in a helper function called `Label` or similar to keep scripts readable.

## 7. Boolean columns require OptionSet with TrueOption/FalseOption, not just a type hint

**Why:** `BooleanAttributeMetadata` demands an `OptionSet` containing both `TrueOption` and `FalseOption`, each with a `Value` (1 and 0) and a `Label`. Without this the create fails.

**How to apply:**

```powershell
@{
  '@odata.type' = 'Microsoft.Dynamics.CRM.BooleanAttributeMetadata'
  AttributeType = 'Boolean'
  AttributeTypeName = @{ Value = 'BooleanType' }
  SchemaName = 'dcfg_Active_Flag'
  DefaultValue = $true
  OptionSet = @{
    '@odata.type' = 'Microsoft.Dynamics.CRM.BooleanOptionSetMetadata'
    TrueOption = @{ Value = 1; Label = (Label 'Active') }
    FalseOption = @{ Value = 0; Label = (Label 'Retired') }
  }
}
```

## 8. Virtual name columns auto-appear for Boolean and Lookup attrs

**Why:** Creating `dcfg_active_flag` (Boolean) automatically creates `dcfg_active_flagname` (Virtual) as a display-label column. Creating `dcfg_customerid` (Lookup) creates `dcfg_customeridname` (String) as a FK display column.

**How to apply:** Don't try to create these manually. Don't try to delete them. They're always there. When listing attributes, filter out `*name` suffix if you want the real columns only.

## 9. Lookup columns are created via RelationshipDefinitions, not Attributes

**Why:** Direct POST to `EntityDefinitions(LogicalName='x')/Attributes` with a `LookupAttributeMetadata` body will either fail or create an unlinked lookup column. The correct path is to create a `OneToManyRelationshipMetadata` on `RelationshipDefinitions`, which creates the lookup AS PART OF the relationship.

**How to apply:**

```powershell
POST /api/data/v9.2/RelationshipDefinitions
{
  '@odata.type': 'Microsoft.Dynamics.CRM.OneToManyRelationshipMetadata',
  SchemaName: 'dcfg_parent_dcfg_child_fieldname',
  ReferencedEntity: 'dcfg_parent',    // the '1' side
  ReferencedAttribute: 'dcfg_parentid',
  ReferencingEntity: 'dcfg_child',    // the 'many' side
  Lookup: {
    '@odata.type': 'Microsoft.Dynamics.CRM.LookupAttributeMetadata',
    SchemaName: 'dcfg_ParentId',
    DisplayName: { ... }
  },
  AssociatedMenuConfiguration: { Behavior: 'UseCollectionName', Group: 'Details', Order: 10000, Label: ... },
  CascadeConfiguration: { Assign: 'NoCascade', Delete: 'RemoveLink', ... }
}
```

